<goToClassText>
#if ($forgotFood) You walk in and have to explain to everyone why you showed up with nothing to offer. You hear a few chuckles and see some rolled eyes as you recount your kitchen disaster. Even the instructor seems annoyed with your forgetfulness. After your story, you pass by the table where the dishes from the other students are set and you see a wide variety of foods represented. Evidently, no two students got the same recipe, which makes the lack of your dish all the more disappointing.
#else You bring your dish to class and set it with the assortment of other foods you see already on a table when you arrive. It appears no two people were given the same recipe, so there's a decent variety of foods represented on the table.
#end##

After everyone has officially settled in, the instructor encourages all of you to sample what the others have brought and give constructive feedback based on your thoughts. You sample a little bit of everything, making sure to give whatever helpful pointers you can.##
#if ($w.getSkill("COOKING") < 20) As a complete amateur, you feel that your advice isn't particularly useful, but you still try to help where you can. You might not know the ins and outs of everything food related, but you can still tell when a dish has too much salt or something didn't cook long enough. You try to give at least one piece of helpful feedback to everyone before while sampling the assortment of food in front of you.
#elseif($w.getSkill("COOKING") < 50) Since you're still relatively inexperienced though, your advice probably isn't that helpful. You can go a bit more in depth in your criticism than someone who's never cooked before, but no one is going to take your feedback as a sign that you should be teaching the class. Regardless, you still try to offer some advice you feel is helpful to everyone whose dishes you sample.##
#else As you're probably one of the more experienced chefs here, your advice is likely some of the most trustworthy. In fact, some people actively seek out your advice upon hearing the feedback that you give to their peers. By the end of the sampling session, while you don't feel that you should be leading the class, you feel much more confident and knowledgeable about cooking than you did when you first entered the room.##
#end##

Once the sampling wraps up and everyone has received feedback on their food, you are encouraged to choose your partner.
</goToClassText>

